

**Example 10.** Evaluate the integral \(\int_{0}^{\ast\ast}\frac{\sin x}{x}\,dx\)\(=\)\(\frac{\pi}{2}\) to 4D using the Euler transform.

\(\int_{0}^{\ast}\frac{\sin x}{x}\,dx\)\(=\)\(\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\ast}\int_{k\ast}^{\alpha+t)\ast}\frac{\sin x}{x}\,dx\)\(=\)\(\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\ast}\int_{0}^{\ast}\frac{\sin\left(k\pi+t\right)}{k\pi+t}\,dt\)\(=\)\(\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\ast}\left(-1\right)^{k}\int_{0}^{\ast}\frac{\sin t}{k\pi+t}\,dt\).

Evaluating the integrals in the last sum by numerical integration we get

\(k\)\(\int_{0}^{\ast}\frac{\sin t}{k\pi+t}\,dt\)\(\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\ast}\frac{\sin t}{k\pi+t}\,dt\)\(\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\ast}\frac{\sin t}{k\pi+t}\,dt\)\(\sum\limits_{k=1}^{\ast}k^{-z}\)\(=\)\(1.54976\,7731\)\(